  "scope": "Registered source assays support gene-associated reaction potential through exact called-protein tuples or independently bound complete-reference CDS encodings at 5-prime boundary differences in a catalytic core or required module. Every encoded member must translate to its complete registered protein under the bound current model; raw query proteins, coordinates, partial flags and independent catalytic identity remain unchanged. Legal initiator normalization and synonymous DNA changes are distinct from amino-acid variants. All local linkage, unique-role and resource-ownership gates remain. No general homolog-function transfer, expression, mature-construct equivalence, physical assembly, cofactor occupancy, query activity, exclusive specificity or in-vivo net direction is asserted. The source-assay bank stays separate from discovery and identity search banks. The coding-locus route requires bound genomic DNA and its calling proof in genome/assembly/bin input contexts, under their existing association rules; a mode label alone supplies no DNA evidence and does not establish whole-genome validation credit. The BBS Hox2 NADH-to-H2 contract is explicitly limited to a mutant-resolved soluble total-protein preparation with added FMN/DTT; it is not a purified-complex electron-transfer or NADPH-exclusion claim. The evidence scope is retained per capability. Source-declared local member groups are permitted only for a registered assay set with a bound source/review. Every exact or independently encoded member remains required; each group retains local linkage and all groups retain the input ownership and unique-resource checks. Without an explicit source declaration the original whole-tuple local linkage applies. A documented dispersed preparation does not establish arbitrary homolog pairing, HydABC-only sufficiency or query physical assembly. A single ambiguous noncatalytic singleton group may be resolved among complete source-equivalent tuples by genomic context and a deterministic ID tie-break; all valid equivalent loci remain explicit alternatives and preferred confidence is medium. Literal query-member assay attribution remains not tested. Multiple-role ambiguity, source identity, ownership and resource conflict gates remain unchanged. When strict source attribution fails, complete source-equivalent chemical components may support a separate sequence-function hypothesis. Only linkage/locality or locus assignment uncertainty may remain after complete tuple proof; catalytic identity, full source sequence/coding proof, coordinates, input ownership and resources remain hard gates. These component reactions have a separate inference scope, never populate exact-source capability fields, and use medium qualitative preferred confidence with explicit pairing and assembly risks. A separately audited FDH role homolog may support coarse formate coupling when every other source backbone member is independently source-equivalent and input/resource ownership holds. It never inherits exact source sequence equality or the measured source reaction details; metal, specificity, joint NAD/Fd mechanism, physical assembly and query activity remain unasserted. A single independently supported noncatalytic module homolog may supply a separate coarse source-function prior in a complete otherwise source-equivalent local tuple. The complete current module/coupling proof and all original tuple gates except that one exact-sequence equality remain required. Global source-sequence comparison has no E-value and does not transfer source assay contracts, reaction details, numerical rates or measured query activity. Source function can outrank a generic template when its query-normalized exact-match support is no weaker than that selected module template; the full role coverage gates still apply independently. Coverage denominators from different references are not a hard preference gate. Otherwise the source function is a low-confidence alternative. A nonexact module source prior inherits low confidence and structural contact risks from its current role proof; sequence similarity ranking cannot erase role uncertainty.",
